---
/**
 * Gated download page for the BC Owner's Handbook.
 *
 * LAUNCH GATE: the handbook describes statutory obligations carrying financial
 * consequences and must not go live until reviewed by a BC construction lawyer.
 * See docs/sources/statutory-verification.md.
 *
 * The email capture posts to Formspree (same endpoint as the project review
 * form, distinguished by the _subject field) and redirects to
 * /handbook/thank-you, which carries the download link.
 */
import Article from '../layouts/Article.astro';
import Plate from '../components/Plate.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';

const chapters: [string, string, string][] = [
  ['01', 'Who works for whom', 'The incentive map of a construction project, described fairly.'],
  ['02', 'Before you sign', 'Fixed price, cost-plus and GMP — and the clauses worth reading twice.'],
  ['03', 'Who is allowed to build your house', 'Licensing, verification, and what owner-builder status costs you.'],
  ['04', 'The warranty you get, and the gaps', '2-5-10 explained properly, including the windows inside the two years.'],
  ['05', 'The 10% you are legally required to withhold', 'The statutory holdback, and what non-compliance exposes you to.'],
  ['06', 'Paying for work', 'What a payment application is, and the four places the number goes wrong.'],
  ['07', 'Change orders', 'Scope, cause, cost — and a log that survives a dispute.'],
  ['08', 'When a lien appears', 'What it does to title, the periods that apply, and what to do first.'],
  ['09', 'Finishing', 'Substantial completion, the holdback period, the title search, closeout.'],
  ['10', 'If you are not there', 'Verification at distance, and why the statutory clocks bite harder.'],
];

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Book',
    name: 'Building in British Columbia: What the Owner Is Responsible For',
    author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: SITE.name, url: SITE.url },
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: SITE.name, url: SITE.url },
    inLanguage: 'en-CA',
    about:
      'Owner obligations under the Builders Lien Act and Homeowner Protection Act in British Columbia',
  },
];
---

<Article
  title={`The BC Owner's Handbook — ${SITE.name}`}
  heading="Building in British Columbia: what the owner is responsible for"
  kicker="A free guide"
  description="A plain-language guide to the obligations British Columbia law places on the owner of a residential construction project — licensing, warranty, the statutory 10% holdback, payment, change orders, liens and completion. Free, with section references throughout."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p>
    British Columbia places obligations directly on the person who owns the land and signs the construction
    contract. Some of them carry money consequences if you get them wrong. Almost none of them are explained
    to you by anyone on the project — not because anyone is hiding them, but because nobody on the project is
    paid to explain them.
  </p>
  <p>
    This guide sets out, in order, what the legislation asks of you, what the paperwork actually means, and
    where the ordinary failure points are. Every statutory claim carries its section number so you or your
    lawyer can read the source. It is written for someone who has never built before and is not looking to be
    impressed.
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    <p>
      An owner can pay every invoice on time, in full, with no complaint about the work — and still be
      required to pay part of it a second time. That mechanism is Chapter 5.
    </p>
  </blockquote>

  <div slot="after" class="mt-14">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">What's in it</h2>

    <ol class="mt-6 max-w-3xl">
      {
        chapters.map(([n, title, blurb]) => (
          <li class="grid grid-cols-[3rem_1fr] border-t border-rule py-5">
            <p class="annotation pt-1">{n}</p>
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-base font-semibold">{title}</h3>
              <p class="mt-1.5 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed text-ink/90">{blurb}</p>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))
      }
      <li class="grid grid-cols-[3rem_1fr] border-t border-b border-rule py-5">
        <p class="annotation pt-1">A–C</p>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-base font-semibold">Appendices</h3>
          <p class="mt-1.5 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed text-ink/90">
            An owner's obligations checklist by project phase, nine questions to ask before signing, and a
            glossary.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <Plate
      src="/plates/plate-07-rule.svg"
      alt="A steel measuring rule on an empty field, casting one shadow"
      caption="Every statutory claim in the guide carries its section reference."
      class="my-12 max-w-3xl aspect-[21/9]"
    />

    <div class="max-w-2xl border-t border-rule pt-10">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Where should we send it?</h2>
      <p class="prose mt-4">
        One email with the guide attached. We do not run a newsletter, we do not sell or share the list, and
        you can ask us to delete your address at any time by replying. See the <a href="/privacy">privacy
        page</a>.
      </p>

      <form name="handbook" method="POST" action={SITE.formEndpoint} class="mt-8">
        <input type="hidden" name="_subject" value="Handbook download — erskineadvisory.com" />
        <input type="hidden" name="_next" value={`${SITE.url}/handbook/thank-you`} />
        <input type="hidden" name="source" value="handbook-gate" />

        <p class="hidden" aria-hidden="true">
          <label>
            Leave this field empty: <input name="_gotcha" tabindex="-1" autocomplete="off" />
          </label>
        </p>

        <div class="grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-2">
          <div>
            <label for="hb-name" class="block text-sm font-medium">
              Name <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent">*</span>
            </label>
            <input
              id="hb-name"
              name="name"
              type="text"
              required
              autocomplete="name"
              class="mt-2 w-full border border-rule bg-white px-3 py-2.5 text-base focus:border-accent"
            />
          </div>
          <div>
            <label for="hb-email" class="block text-sm font-medium">
              Email <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent">*</span>
            </label>
            <input
              id="hb-email"
              name="email"
              type="email"
              required
              autocomplete="email"
              class="mt-2 w-full border border-rule bg-white px-3 py-2.5 text-base focus:border-accent"
            />
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-6">
          <label for="hb-role" class="block text-sm font-medium">
            Your role <span class="text-mid">(optional)</span>
          </label>
          <select
            id="hb-role"
            name="role"
            class="mt-2 w-full max-w-sm border border-rule bg-white px-3 py-2.5 text-base focus:border-accent"
          >
            <option value="">Select one</option>
            <option value="owner">Owner — building or planning to</option>
            <option value="family-office">Family office or private staff</option>
            <option value="architect">Architect or designer</option>
            <option value="lawyer">Lawyer</option>
            <option value="other">Other</option>
          </select>
        </div>

        <button
          type="submit"
          class="mt-8 border border-ink bg-ink px-6 py-3 text-base font-medium text-paper transition-colors hover:border-accent hover:bg-accent"
        >
          Send me the handbook
        </button>
      </form>

      <p class="mt-8 max-w-prose text-sm leading-relaxed text-mid">
        This guide explains what the legislation requires. It is not legal advice. Consult a BC construction
        lawyer on your specific project.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Article>
